Fill an opaque Wayland window's native-view holes with the colour the views ask for, under the views.

What: in an opaque window whose views ask for a colour (`NativeView::set_background`, core's
`native_view_placement::background`), GPUI's own underlay subsurface, bottom of the stack, shows it across the views'
bounds (a 1×1 `wp_single_pixel_buffer_v1` buffer, else shm, scaled by a viewport), so a hole never shows the desktop
where a view hasn't drawn. A see-through window shows what is behind it, as on Windows. `client.rs` binds
`wp_single_pixel_buffer_manager_v1` when offered. Table test: `frames_stack_views_in_paint_order_over_an_underlay`
(the underlay is new code, not from Chromium).

Why: before a view's first buffer, when it shows again, or while it catches up with a resize, its hole would show the
desktop through an opaque window.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

